items.body:
The `mha_fwd_kvcache` function contains this GQA optimization that triggers whenever `seqlen_q` is 1, with a few other conditions:

```c++
    // Faster to transpose q from (b, 1, (nheads_kv ngroups), d) to (b, ngroups, nheads_kv, d) in this case
    // H/t Daniel Haziza
    const int seqlenq_ngroups_swapped = seqlen_q == 1 && num_heads > num_heads_k && window_size_left < 0 && window_size_right < 0 && head_size_og % 8 == 0 && !alibi_slopes_.has_value();
    if (seqlenq_ngroups_swapped) {
        const int ngroups = num_heads / num_heads_k;
        q = q.reshape({batch_size, num_heads_k, ngroups, head_size_og}).transpose(1, 2);
        seqlen_q = ngroups;
        num_heads = num_heads_k;
    }
```

Meanwhile, when passing an output tensor to the function, it's expected to match these modified dimensions:

```c++
    at::Tensor out;
    if (out_.has_value()) {
        out = out_.value();
        TORCH_CHECK(out.dtype() == q_dtype, "Output must have the same dtype as inputs");
        CHECK_DEVICE(out);
        TORCH_CHECK(out.stride(-1) == 1, "Output tensor must have contiguous last dimension");
        CHECK_SHAPE(out, batch_size, seqlen_q, num_heads, head_size_og);
        if (head_size_og % 8 != 0) { out = torch::empty_like(q_padded); }
    } else {
        out = torch::empty_like(q_padded);
    }
```

.. resulting in this error:

```
RuntimeError: out must have shape (batch_size, seqlen_q, num_heads, head_size_og)
```

Even though the `out` tensor does have that shape when passed to the function.

It took a little while to figure out why I was getting this error, since the message from `CHECK_SHAPE` is sort of misleading. I'm not sure if the optimization should be disabled when `out_` is set, or if it should also temporarily reshape the `out_` tensor (I could imagine issues with that if `out_` is a slice of a static buffer.)

In the meantime, I'm currently getting around it by passing a very high value in `window_size_left`, which causes it to skip the optimization, and as long as the value is high enough it gets reset anyway, here:

```c++
    if (window_size_left >= seqlen_k) { window_size_left = -1; }
```

If this isn't actually an oversight, is there a cleaner way I could be doing it?
